[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Transaction Isolation Levels in SQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transaction isolation levels control the degree to which transactions are isolated from one another. They define the level of protection from the effects of concurrent transactions. Higher isolation levels increase data consistency but can reduce concurrency. SQL defines four standard isolation levels: Read Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read, and Serializable. Each level addresses different concurrency issues like dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Read Uncommitted"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Read Uncommitted is the lowest isolation level. In this level, a transaction can read data that has been modified by another transaction but not yet committed. This can lead to 'dirty reads', where a transaction reads data that is later rolled back, resulting in incorrect data."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Dirty Reads"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"A dirty read occurs when a transaction reads data that has been modified by another transaction but not yet committed. If the modifying transaction rolls back, the reading transaction will have read invalid data."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ UNCOMMITTED;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE account_id = 1;

-- Transaction 2 (Connection 2)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ UNCOMMITTED;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SELECT balance FROM Accounts WHERE account_id = 1; -- Reads the uncommitted value

-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;

-- Transaction 2 (Connection 2)
-- The value read was incorrect because Transaction 1 rolled back."},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, Transaction 2 reads the uncommitted balance change made by Transaction 1. If Transaction 1 rolls back, Transaction 2 has read incorrect data. Use Read Uncommitted only when absolutely necessary due to its potential for data inconsistency."},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"2. Read Committed"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Read Committed is a higher isolation level than Read Uncommitted. It guarantees that a transaction only reads data that has been committed. This prevents dirty reads. However, it is still susceptible to 'non-repeatable reads'."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Non-Repeatable Reads"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"A non-repeatable read occurs when a transaction reads the same row twice, but the row has been modified and committed by another transaction between the two reads. This results in the transaction seeing different values for the same row."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SELECT balance FROM Accounts WHERE account_id = 1;

-- Transaction 2 (Connection 2)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Accounts SET balance = balance - 50 WHERE account_id = 1;
COMMIT TRANSACTION;

-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
SELECT balance FROM Accounts WHERE account_id = 1; -- Reads a different value than the first read
COMMIT TRANSACTION;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, Transaction 1 reads the balance. Transaction 2 then updates and commits the balance. When Transaction 1 reads the balance again, it sees a different value. Read Committed is a common default isolation level because it avoids dirty reads while allowing a reasonable level of concurrency."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"3. Repeatable Read"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Repeatable Read provides a higher level of isolation than Read Committed. It guarantees that within a transaction, any rows read will not be changed by other transactions. This prevents both dirty reads and non-repeatable reads. However, it is still susceptible to 'phantom reads'."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Phantom Reads"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"A phantom read occurs when a transaction executes the same query twice, but another transaction has inserted new rows that satisfy the query conditions between the two executions. This results in the first transaction seeing 'phantom' rows that were not present during the first query."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE customer_id = 123;

-- Transaction 2 (Connection 2)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Orders (customer_id, order_date) VALUES (123, GETDATE());
COMMIT TRANSACTION;

-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE customer_id = 123; -- May see a new row (phantom read)
COMMIT TRANSACTION;"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, Transaction 1 selects orders for a customer. Transaction 2 inserts a new order for the same customer. When Transaction 1 re-executes the select statement, it may see the new order, which is a phantom read. Repeatable Read provides stronger consistency than Read Committed but can still lead to unexpected results due to phantom reads."},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"4. Serializable"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Serializable is the highest isolation level. It guarantees that transactions are completely isolated from each other. It prevents dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads. Serializable achieves this by placing a lock on the data being read, preventing other transactions from modifying or inserting data that would affect the current transaction's results. This level provides the highest level of data consistency but can significantly reduce concurrency."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SELECT * FROM Products WHERE category = 'Electronics';

-- Transaction 2 (Connection 2)
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

INSERT INTO Products (name, category) VALUES ('New Gadget', 'Electronics'); -- May be blocked until Transaction 1 completes
COMMIT TRANSACTION;

-- Transaction 1 (Connection 1)
COMMIT TRANSACTION;"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, Transaction 1 selects all products in the 'Electronics' category. Transaction 2 attempts to insert a new product into the same category. Because Transaction 1 is using Serializable isolation, Transaction 2's insert operation might be blocked until Transaction 1 completes, preventing a phantom read. Serializable provides the strongest consistency but can have a significant impact on performance due to increased locking."},{"order":25,"type":"heading","text":"Summary Table"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's a table summarizing the isolation levels and the concurrency issues they prevent:"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Read Uncommitted:** Allows dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Read Committed:** Prevents dirty reads, but allows non-repeatable reads and phantom reads."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Repeatable Read:** Prevents dirty reads and non-repeatable reads, but allows phantom reads."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Serializable:** Prevents dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads."},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the appropriate isolation level involves balancing data consistency with concurrency requirements. Higher isolation levels provide greater data consistency but can reduce concurrency, while lower isolation levels allow for greater concurrency but may result in data inconsistencies. The specific needs of the application should guide the selection of the appropriate isolation level."}]
